    }
    duration { min 2.1.days, mode 7.days, max 14.days }
    valence good
  }
  amended-12b-motion-filed "File pre-answer Rule 12(b) motion instead of answering" -> motion-to-dismiss-filed {
    authority cite "FRCP 12(b)"
    effort 30.hours
    cost 8_000.usd
    within amended-response-period {
      extendable true
      extension_authority "FRCP 6(b) stipulation or court order"
      note "Filing the motion extends the responsive-pleading deadline to 14 days after the court denies or postpones disposition (Rule 12(a)(4)(A))."
    }
    duration { min 2.1.days, mode 7.days, max 14.days }
    note "This pack doesn't separately model which Rule 12(b) defenses a motion raises, so sets waived-12h-defenses on every 12(b) motion as the conservative default: FRCP 12(h)(1)(A) waives lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, and insufficient service of process by omitting any of them from a motion made under the circumstances described in Rule 12(g)(2) (a motion raising some, but not all, available Rule 12 defenses). A pack that modeled motion content in more detail could instead set this flag only on the omitting variant."
    sets [waived-12h-defenses]
  }
  edge-amended-response-window-default-entered "Fail to answer or move -- default exposure" -> default-entered {
    authority cite "FRCP 55(a)"
    effort 0.hours
    valence bad
  }
}

state answer-filed: "Answer Filed" by applicant cite "FRCP 8(b); 8(c); 12(a)(4)(A); 13"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "The answer admits, denies, or states lack of knowledge as to each allegation (Rule 8(b)), affirmatively pleads any avoidance or affirmative defense such as statute of limitations, waiver, or accord and satisfaction (Rule 8(c)(1)) -- omitting one without later amendment forfeits it -- and may assert compulsory counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence (Rule 13(a)) or permissive counterclaims (Rule 13(b)). Compulsory counterclaims not pleaded here are generally barred from later, separate litigation."
  edge-answer-filed-jury-demand-decision "Last pleading directed to the issue served -- 14-day jury demand clock starts" -> jury-demand-decision {
    authority cite "FRCP 38(b)"
    effort 0.hours
    note "Hours: 0, a default/no-action transition with no billable work of its own."
  }
}

state motion-to-dismiss-filed: "Rule 12(b) Motion Filed" by applicant cite "FRCP 12(b)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "Seven enumerated defenses may be raised by motion instead of the answer: (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, (2) lack of personal jurisdiction, (3) improper venue, (4) insufficient process, (5) insufficient service of process, (6) failure to state a claim, (7) failure to join a required party. THE OMNIBUS TRAP: Rule 12(g)(2) bars a second Rule 12 motion raising a defense that was available but omitted from the first; Rule 12(h)(1) then WAIVES defenses (2)-(5) if omitted from both the first motion and the answer. Defenses (6) and (7) survive through trial (Rule 12(h)(2)); lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (1) can never be waived (Rule 12(h)(3))."
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/145730/bell-atlantic-corp-v-twombly/"
  edge-motion-to-dismiss-filed-mtd-ruling "Complete Rule 12(b) briefing" -> mtd-ruling {
    authority cite "FRCP 12(b)"
    effort 20.hours
    cost 6_000.usd
  }
}

chance mtd-ruling: "Court Rules on Motion to Dismiss" by examiner discretion cite "FRCP 12(b)(6); 12(d)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "Outcome distribution authored at 0.45 deny / 0.35 grant-with-leave / 0.20 grant-without-leave -- APPROXIMATE, not a live empirical figure; district courts deny more 12(b)(6) motions than they grant outright, and leave to amend is the norm rather than the exception on a first dismissal (Foman v. Davis, 371 U.S. 178 (1962): leave should be 'freely given'). If matters outside the pleadings are presented and not excluded, the motion converts to one for summary judgment under Rule 56 (Rule 12(d)) -- not separately modeled here."
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/145875/ashcroft-v-iqbal/"
  edge-mtd-ruling-answer-filed "Motion denied -- answer due within 14 days" -> answer-filed p 0.45 {
    authority cite "FRCP 12(a)(4)(A)"
    within denial-answer-period {
      extendable true
      extension_authority "FRCP 6(b) stipulation or court order"
    }
    duration { min 2.1.days, mode 7.days, max 14.days }
  }
  edge-mtd-ruling-amended-complaint-filed "Motion granted with leave to amend" -> amended-complaint-filed p 0.35 {
    authority cite "FRCP 15(a)(2)"
  }
  edge-mtd-ruling-dismissed-with-prejudice "Motion granted without leave to amend -- futility" -> dismissed-with-prejudice p 0.2 {
    authority cite "FRCP 12(b)(6); 41(b)"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-with-prejudice: "DISMISSED WITH PREJUDICE" by applicant outcome [loss, dismissal, waiver-trap] cite "FRCP 12(b)(6); 41(b)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "PERMADEATH. The complaint fails to state a claim and the court concludes amendment would be futile -- dismissal without leave to amend. Rule 41(b)'s final sentence extends beyond its own subdivision: unless the dismissal order states otherwise, 'any dismissal not under this rule -- except one for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a party under Rule 19 -- operates as an adjudication on the merits,' which is what makes a with-prejudice 12(b)(6) dismissal claim-preclusive. PLACEHOLDER PAYOFF: $0, override per matter."
  valence bad
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/769838/john-andrew-cuoco-v-kenneth-moritsugu/"
  payoff 0.usd
  dismissal-appeal-clock "Dismissal is a final decision -- the losing party weighs an appeal" -> notice-of-appeal-window {
    authority cite "28 U.S.C. 1291; FRAP 4(a)(1)(A)"
    effort 0.hours
    note "A dismissal with prejudice disposes of the whole action, so it is a final decision appealable under 28 U.S.C. 1291 and the FRAP 4(a)(1)(A) clock runs from entry of the judgment (Twombly: S.D.N.Y. dismissal, 313 F. Supp. 2d 174 (2003), appealed to the Second Circuit, 425 F.3d 99 (2005)). Hours: 0, a no-action transition; the filing itself is the notice-of-appeal edge."
  }
}

decision default-entered: "Clerk's Entry of Default" by applicant cite "FRCP 55(a)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "When a party against whom relief is sought has failed to plead or otherwise defend, and that failure is shown by affidavit or otherwise, the clerk MUST enter default. This is purely clerical and does not itself award judgment. A defaulted defendant may move to set aside the entry for good cause (Rule 55(c)) -- courts apply a lenient standard here, distinct from the stricter Rule 60(b) standard for setting aside a final default JUDGMENT."
  edge-default-entered-answer-filed "Move to set aside entry of default for good cause" -> answer-filed {
    authority cite "FRCP 55(c)"
    effort 10.hours
    cost 3_500.usd
    valence good
  }
  edge-default-entered-default-judgment-entered "Clerk or court enters default judgment" -> default-judgment-entered by office {
    authority cite "FRCP 55(b)(1); 55(b)(2)"
  }
}

terminal default-judgment-entered: "DEFAULT JUDGMENT ENTERED" outcome [win, default-judgment] cite "FRCP 55(b)(1); 55(b)(2)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "PERMADEATH (absent a successful Rule 60(b) motion, not separately modeled from this terminal). The clerk enters judgment for a sum certain if the defendant has not appeared and is not a minor or incompetent (55(b)(1)); otherwise the court enters it, and may hold a hearing to determine damages, establish the truth of an allegation, or investigate any other matter (55(b)(2)). Well-pleaded factual allegations (other than damages) are taken as true against the defaulting party -- damages must still be proven. PLACEHOLDER PAYOFF: $900,000, override per matter. Entered against a non-appearing defendant -- a real win for the plaintiff (self), discounted from a full $1,000,000 because it was never tested on the merits. The pack's authored `valence` of 'bad' reflects how disfavored/drastic default judgments are procedurally, not who benefits -- flagged since it runs the other way from self's payoff."
  valence bad
  payoff 900_000.usd
}

terminal voluntary-dismissal: "VOLUNTARY / STIPULATED DISMISSAL" outcome [settlement, voluntary] cite "FRCP 41(a)"
{
  group pleading-group
  note "Plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice of dismissal before the opposing party serves either an answer or a summary-judgment motion (41(a)(1)(A)(i)), or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared (41(a)(1)(A)(ii)) -- the common vehicle for a negotiated settlement. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order, typically on terms the court considers proper (41(a)(2)). A first 41(a)(1) dismissal is without prejudice unless the notice/stipulation states otherwise; a SECOND dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits (the 'two-dismissal rule,' 41(a)(1)(B)) -- not itself further modeled here. PLACEHOLDER PAYOFF: $400,000, override per matter, matching this project's settlement tier -- per this terminal's own note, 'the common vehicle for a negotiated settlement.'"
  valence good
  payoff 400_000.usd
}

decision amendment-as-of-right-window: "Rule 15(a)(1) Amendment-as-of-Right Window" by applicant cite "FRCP 15(a)(1)(B)"
{
  group amendment-joinder-group
  note "A party may amend once as a matter of course no later than 21 days after service of a responsive pleading, or 21 days after service of a Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier -- the two triggers run independently and the earlier one controls. Miss both 21-day windows and Rule 15(a)(1) is exhausted; amendment thereafter requires the opposing party's written consent or the court's leave, which the court 'should freely give ... when justice so requires' (15(a)(2))."
  edge-amendment-as-of-right-window-amended-complaint-filed "Amend as of right within the 21-day matter-of-course window" -> amended-complaint-filed {
    authority cite "FRCP 15(a)(1)(B)"
    effort 8.hours
    cost 2_500.usd
    within amendment-as-of-course-period {
      extendable true
      extension_authority "FRCP 6(b)(1): for good cause before the period expires, or on motion after it for excusable neglect"
      note "21 days after service of the responsive pleading, or 21 days after service of a Rule 12(b)/(e)/(f) motion, whichever is earlier. The court may extend it under FRCP 6(b)(1) (Rule 15(a)(1) is not on the 6(b)(2) list); missing it unextended forfeits the matter-of-course right, leaving consent or leave under Rule 15(a)(2)."
    }
    duration { min 3.1.days, mode 10.5.days, max 21.days }
  }
  edge-amendment-as-of-right-window-leave-to-amend-motion "Seek leave to amend outside the matter-of-course window" -> leave-to-amend-motion {
    authority cite "FRCP 15(a)(2)"
    effort 6.hours
    cost 3_000.usd
  }
  edge-amendment-as-of-right-window-joinder-decision "No amendment -- proceed; consider joinder of parties" -> joinder-decision {
    authority cite "FRCP 19; 20; 24"
    effort 0.hours
    note "Hours: 0, a default/no-action transition with no billable work of its own."
  }
}

state amended-complaint-filed: "Amended Complaint Filed" by office cite "FRCP 15(a); 15(c)(1)(C)"
{
  group amendment-joinder-group
  note "An amendment changing the party against whom a claim is asserted relates back to the original filing date only if it arises from the same conduct/transaction/occurrence AND, within the Rule 4(m) service period, the new party received notice avoiding prejudice and knew or should have known the action would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning identity (Rule 15(c)(1)(C)). Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A., 560 U.S. 538 (2010): relation-back turns on what the NEW PARTY knew or should have known, not on the diligence of the amending plaintiff."
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/147952/krupski-v-costa-crociere-s-p-a/"
  edge-amended-complaint-filed-amended-response-window "Responsive-pleading window reopens for the amended pleading" -> amended-response-window {
    authority cite "FRCP 15(a)(3)"
    within amended-response-period {
      extendable true
      extension_authority "FRCP 6(b) stipulation or court order"
      note "Response due within the time remaining to respond to the original pleading, or 14 days after service of the amended pleading, whichever is LATER."
    }
    duration { min 2.1.days, mode 7.days, max 14.days }
  }
}

