{
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "PERMADEATH for the losing party's claims/defenses on the merits (subject to appeal). No genuine dispute of material fact exists and the movant -- plaintiff or defendant -- is entitled to judgment as a matter of law on the full case. A grant of summary judgment is reviewed de novo on appeal, unlike a jury verdict's deferential review. PLACEHOLDER PAYOFF: $500,000 (stake midpoint), override per matter. This pack does not branch on which party a judgment favors -- `verdict-rendered`'s 'for plaintiff'/'for defendant' edges both lead to the same `final-judgment-entered` state, and every downstream terminal (summary judgment, appeal outcomes) tracks only the procedural posture from there, not who won. A single payoff here necessarily blends a possible plaintiff win with a possible plaintiff loss; override with scenario.payoffs once the matter's actual judgment is known."
  valence good
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/145738/scott-v-harris/"
  payoff 500_000.usd
  summary-judgment-appeal-clock "Summary judgment disposing of the case is a final decision -- the losing party weighs an appeal" -> notice-of-appeal-window {
    authority cite "28 U.S.C. 1291; FRAP 4(a)(1)(A)"
    effort 0.hours
    note "Summary judgment on the full case is a final decision appealable under 28 U.S.C. 1291; the FRAP 4(a)(1)(A) clock runs from entry of judgment (Celotex: D.D.C. summary judgment, July 1982, reversed by the D.C. Circuit, 756 F.2d 181 (1985)). A partial summary judgment is not covered here: it is interlocutory absent FRCP 54(b) certification. Hours: 0, a no-action transition."
  }
}

state partial-summary-judgment: "Partial Summary Judgment" by applicant cite "FRCP 56(a); 56(g)"
{
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "Some claims or defenses are resolved as a matter of law; the remainder proceeds toward trial. If the court does not grant all requested relief, it may enter an order stating any material fact -- including an item of damages or other relief -- that is not genuinely in dispute, treating that fact as established for the rest of the case (Rule 56(g))."
  edge-partial-summary-judgment-daubert-motion-filed "Proceed toward trial on the remaining claims" -> daubert-motion-filed {
    authority cite ?("FRE 702")
    effort 0.hours
    note "Hours: 0, a default/no-action transition with no billable work of its own."
  }
}

state daubert-motion-filed: "Daubert / Rule 702 Motion Filed" by applicant cite ?("FRE 702; FRE 104(a)") {
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "The proponent of expert testimony must show by a preponderance of the evidence (2023 amendment to FRE 702 made this explicit) that the witness's specialized knowledge will help the trier of fact, the testimony is based on sufficient facts/data, is the product of reliable principles/methods, and reflects a reliable application of those principles to the case's facts. The court decides this preliminary admissibility question itself under FRE 104(a), not the jury -- a pretrial gatekeeping motion, not a trial objection."
  edge-daubert-motion-filed-daubert-ruling "Complete FRE 702 gatekeeping briefing (FRE 104(a))" -> daubert-ruling {
    authority cite ?("FRE 702; FRE 104(a)")
    effort 20.hours
  }
}

// The rule text commits this ruling to the court's discretion.
chance daubert-ruling: "Court Rules on Daubert Motion" by examiner discretion cite ?("FRE 702") {
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "Outcome distribution authored at 0.6 admitted / 0.4 excluded-in-whole-or-part -- APPROXIMATE. Missing the scheduling-order's expert-motion cutoff forfeits a pretrial Daubert challenge entirely -- a mid-trial objection to qualified expert testimony is ordinarily too late and the jury has already heard the opinion, the trap this pack's frcp-fragment rulepack flags as fre/daubert-not-moved."
  daubert-admitted "Expert testimony admitted -- reliability and fit satisfied" -> motions-in-limine-filed p 0.6 {
    authority cite ?("FRE 702")
  }
  daubert-excluded "Expert testimony excluded in whole or in part" -> motions-in-limine-filed p 0.4 {
    authority cite ?("FRE 702")
  }
}

state motions-in-limine-filed: "Motions in Limine" by applicant cite ?("FRE 103(d)") {
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "Pretrial motions to admit or exclude specific categories of evidence before trial begins, avoiding prejudicial suggestion to the jury. A ruling on a motion in limine is often preliminary and non-definitive; FRE 103(b) requires the losing party to RENEW its objection when the evidence is actually offered at trial to preserve the issue for appeal absent a definitive ruling on the record (Luce v. United States, 469 U.S. 38 (1984)) -- failing to renew forfeits appellate review of a non-definitive ruling."
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111284/luce-v-united-states/"
  edge-motions-in-limine-filed-final-pretrial-conference "Complete motions in limine (FRE 103(d))" -> final-pretrial-conference {
    authority cite ?("FRE 103(d)")
    effort 10.hours
    cost 4_000.usd
  }
}

state final-pretrial-conference: "Final Pretrial Conference" by office cite "FRCP 16(e)"
{
  group pretrial-motions-group
  note "The court holds a final pretrial conference to formulate a trial plan, including a plan for admitting evidence. The resulting pretrial order controls the course of the action thereafter and may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice."
  edge-final-pretrial-conference-trial-commences "Court enters final pretrial order" -> trial-commences {
    authority cite "FRCP 16(e)"
  }
}

decision jury-demand-decision: "Jury Demand Window" by applicant cite "FRCP 38(b); 38(d)"
{
  group trial-group
  note "Any party may demand a jury trial by serving a written demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the triable issue is served. THE TRAP: a party waives a jury trial unless the demand is properly served AND filed (38(d)) -- this fires right after the answer, well before discovery closes, and is easy to let lapse while attention is on the merits. Only Rule 39(b) discretionary relief remains after a lapse, and courts are not required to grant it."
  court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/105889/beacon-theatres-inc-v-westover/"
  jury-demand-timely "Serve and file timely written jury demand" -> amendment-as-of-right-window {
    authority cite "FRCP 38(b)"
    effort 0.5.hours
    cost 0.usd
    within jury-demand-period {
      extendable true
      extension_authority "FRCP 6(b) good cause"
      note "14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served."
    }
    duration { min 2.1.days, mode 7.days, max 14.days }
    valence good
  }
  jury-demand-waived "Fail to demand within 14 days -- jury trial waived" -> amendment-as-of-right-window {
    authority cite "FRCP 38(d)"
    effort 0.hours
    valence bad
  }
}

state trial-commences: "Trial Commences" by office cite "FRCP 43; 50(a)"
{
  group trial-group
  note "Witness testimony is taken in open court unless a federal statute, the Federal Rules of Evidence, or another FRCP provision provides otherwise. At the close of the evidence on an issue (or of all the evidence), any party may move for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(a) -- a Rule 50(a) motion made BEFORE the case is submitted to the jury is the mandatory prerequisite for later renewing the motion under Rule 50(b); skipping it forecloses post-trial JMOL entirely, though a Rule 59 new-trial motion remains available."
  edge-trial-commences-verdict-rendered "Evidence closed -- case submitted to the factfinder" -> verdict-rendered {
    authority cite "FRCP 50(a)"
  }
}

chance verdict-rendered: "Verdict / Findings Rendered" by examiner discretion cite "FRCP 52(a); 58"
{
  group trial-group
  note "For a bench trial, the court must find the facts specially and state its conclusions of law separately (Rule 52(a)(1)). For a jury trial, the jury returns its verdict. Either way, judgment must be set out in a separate document (Rule 58(a)) -- the FRAP 4 appeal clock and the Rule 50(b)/59 clocks both run from ENTRY of that judgment, with a 150-day backstop under FRAP 4(a)(7)(A)(ii) if no separate document is ever entered."
  verdict-for-plaintiff "Verdict / findings for plaintiff" -> final-judgment-entered p 0.5 {
    authority cite "FRCP 52(a); 58"
  }
  verdict-for-defendant "Verdict / findings for defendant" -> final-judgment-entered p 0.5 {
    authority cite "FRCP 52(a); 58"
  }
}
